{
  "dom_id": "step-q129286",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192535/assets/1969166670ad908b426c49b4f302a1b56b17693a09d97ddcd64c40ba5dc44987.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f(x),$ shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "The function is defined for values less than $-2,$ and for values between $-2$ and $3.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=-2,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is an open circle at $x=3,$ this point is excluded from the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,3).\n$$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=-2,$  even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=-2$ is $1.$",
